{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310732,"nonce":"mhKyylaagKRrvQbv0OBVXumuOXg=","title":"STILL OPEN. Flaky SIGTRAP (exit 133) in test_exception. ~38-41% rate (n=125 over 2026-05-31 measurements). PRIOR DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG — ...","message":"Two partial findings landed; neither closes the flake:\n\n1. **Diagnosis correction**: prior memo claimed failures concentrate on \"test 2 / MyException polymorphic catch / 2nd throw\". Workflow `wbndzlgt6` Phase 0 measured n=13 distribution: tests 2/3/5/6 fail, with failures CONCENTRATED on LATER tests. Pattern is **state accumulation through the test suite**, not a specific polymorphic-catch trigger. The \"first user-defined subclass\" theory does not hold.\n\n2. **Apple crash report ground truth**: libmalloc `\"BUG IN CLIENT: memory corruption of free block\"` — write-after-free / double-free, NOT leak or OOM. Backtrace points into the stack-trace rendering path called from `e.details()`.\n\n3. **Shadow-stack leak (fixed in 38a9bb1)** was real but NOT the cause. Pre-fix rate 41.5% (27/65), post-fix rate 38.3% (23/60), z-test p=0.715 — statistically indistinguishable.\n\n\n`runtime/src/native_stack_trace.rs:551-568` (`make_haxe_string` or equivalent) — `Box::into_raw` with no balancing `Box::from_raw`. Asymmetric ownership across the `haxe_string_free` boundary matches the \"memory corruption of free block\" signature far better than leak pressure.\n\n\nAudit confirmed the asymmetry was real: every `*mut HaxeString` reaching `haxe_string_free` comes from a `Box::into_raw(Box::new(..))` producer (make_haxe_string, haxe_sys ~40 sites, replace/split substrings), but the sink only deallocated the inner byte buffer — the 24-byte header was leaked AND its `ptr` field kept dangling. That's a real 24 B/string leak + UAF window + latent double-free.\n\nPhase 1 patch at `runtime/src/haxe_string.rs:533-588` symmetrises the ownership:\n- Snapshot buffer ptr/cap\n- Poison the header fields in place\n- Dealloc the buffer with its align-1 Layout\n- `drop(Box::from_raw(s))` reclaims the 24-byte header\n\nBut 30-iteration measurement: **11/30 = 36.7% post-fix vs 38% (12/30) baseline** — statistically indistinguishable. The Box-header asymmetry was real but is NOT the dominant trigger.\n\n\n`exception.rs:140-145` `throw_with_message → make_haxe_string → haxe_box_reference_ptr`. The boxed payload pointer's ownership interleaves with `rayzor_get_exception` cleanup on the second throw's MyException polymorphic catch. That path is unchanged by `67bfa91` and is now the strongest hypothesis.\n\n\n\n```sh\nrm -rf .rayzor/blade/cache .rayzor/cache\nMallocStackLogging=1 MallocScribble=1 MallocGuardEdges=1 \\\n  ./target/release/rayzor run compiler/tests/haxe/test_exception.hx\n\n```\n\n\n`./target/release/rayzor run compiler/tests/haxe/test_exception.hx` SIGTRAPs (exit 133) intermittently. Output stops after the first trace (\"basic error\"). Failure rate in isolation:\n\n- Without ariadne stack formatter: 3/10\n- With ariadne stack formatter: ~5/10 (slightly higher; ariadne adds throw-time work that shifts timing)\n\n`./run_haxe_tests.sh` therefore reports 127/128 some runs, 128/128 others. The other tests are stable.\n\nThe flake stops at test 2:\n```haxe\ntry {\n    throw new MyException(\"custom error\");  // ← crashes here intermittently\n} catch(e:Exception) {                       //   polymorphic catch via parent\n    trace(e.message);\n}\n```\n\nTest 1 (`throw new Exception(...); catch(e:Exception)`) always succeeds. Test 2 is the first polymorphic catch (subclass thrown, parent caught). MyException extends Exception, super(msg) wiring works for the message field. The crash happens before `trace(e.message)` runs.\n\n\nSIGTRAP (signal 5) on macOS typically points to a debug `unreachable!` / `debug_assert!` in cranelift JIT or runtime, OR an unaligned-access / breakpoint instruction. The fact that it ONLY happens on the second throw (not the first), and ONLY for the user-defined subclass (Exception base class works), suggests something in the polymorphic catch's RTTI/typeid resolution path is racy or dependent on uninitialised state. Candidates:\n\n- `cleanup_all_scopes` / InsertFreePass freeing the MyException before the catch handler reads `message` (use-after-free that *usually* hits valid memory).\n- Vtable / class metadata registration timing in `__vtable_init__` vs first MyException instantiation.\n- Throw-side stack capture interacting with the exception's own stack-field GEP store.\n\n\n```bash\nfor i in $(seq 1 10); do\n  rm -rf .rayzor/blade/cache .rayzor/cache\n  ./target/release/rayzor run compiler/tests/haxe/test_exception.hx \u003e /dev/null 2\u003e\u00261\n  echo \"Run $i: $?\"\ndone\n```\n\nWhen happy, all are `0`. When unhappy, several are `133` and the run's output truncates at \"basic error\".\n\n\nDon't treat a single failed `./run_haxe_tests.sh` run as a regression for `test_exception` — re-run before chasing. When debugging an unrelated change, this test will sometimes show false-positive failure. Investigate ONLY changes that increase the failure rate substantially (e.g., 9/10) or that change the failure mode (different output, different signal).\n\nTo actually fix: instrument the MyException Throw lowering, watch for use-after-free on the exception object during the parent-class catch dispatch. Likely related to [[bugs-cleanup-scopes-frees-captures]] but for the exception-object lifetime through the unwind path.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_test_exception_flake` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}